  # ── WORKING-STYLE CHECK — THE PERCOLATOR (appended; routed from the hub
  # ask's last arm). Prose Opus 4.6 wired verbatim (wsc-prose brief,
  # 2026-08-20). The pass narration is the Unchipped Mug's origin — the
  # cosmetic itself unlocks off check_freshpot_passed in cosmetics.js, no
  # give_item needed. Janet is safe to speak here: the hub only serves once
  # the party exists, and Janet is the first recruit (act5_trigger).

  @ws_freshpot_pass
  set check_freshpot_done
  set check_freshpot_passed
  Narrator: The break room coffee machine is a 2009 Bunn VPR Series with a grudge and a record of seven confirmed casualties. Andrew approaches it the way certain monks approach silence — not with force, but with conditions.
  Narrator: He runs water through the empty basket first. He waits ninety seconds. He uses the third shelf's filters, not the ones by the sink. He does not press the button twice.
  Janet: How long have you known how to do that?
  Andrew: It just needs time. Everything does, really.
  Narrator: The team drinks actual coffee for the first time since the machine was installed. The mug no one has ever claimed, the white one without a single chip, is in Andrew's hands now. Nobody offered it. Nobody needed to.
  goto n0

  @ws_freshpot_fail
  set check_freshpot_done
  Narrator: Andrew presses the button twice. The machine — a 2009 Bunn VPR Series that has outlasted seven employees and two department reorganizations — produces a liquid that possesses two of coffee's four essential qualities. It is hot, and it is brown.
  Andrew: That's — I think that's coffee.
  Janet: Generous.
  Narrator: The team drinks it anyway, which is, somehow, worse than if they had refused.
  goto n0

scene restructuring_combat
  Restructuring Analyst: Ah. Andrew, is it? I've heard about you. The 'disruptive element.'
  Restructuring Analyst: I've been reviewing your department's metrics. Your efficiency ratio is 0.34. Industry standard is 0.78.
  Andrew: You can't reduce trust administration to a ratio.
  Restructuring Analyst: Everything can be reduced to a ratio. People. Departments. The reduction is the part I'm best at.
  Restructuring Analyst: Your number is up.
  fight restructuring_analyst
  end

scene restructuring_defeated
  Restructuring Analyst: This is... not in my efficiency model. Human variables. Always the hardest to account for.
  Restructuring Analyst: You know Meredith won't stop. She's been planning this for months. The Regional Manager promised her this branch.
  set restructuring_defeated
  end

scene brand_consultant_combat
  Brand Consultant: Oh! Perfect timing. I'm redesigning your department's identity. Trust is SO last decade.
  Brand Consultant: We're pivoting to 'Wealth Solutions.' Or maybe 'Asset Synergy Partners.' I'm still workshopping.
  Andrew: We're a trust department. We manage trusts. The name is fine.
  Brand Consultant: 'Fine' is the ENEMY of 'brand excellence.' Let me show you the mood board.
  Narrator: The mood board is just a photo of a sunset with the word 'DISRUPT' in Helvetica.
  Brand Consultant: You don't appreciate art. Or vision. Or mid-century modern fonts.
  fight brand_consultant
  end

scene brand_consultant_defeated
  Brand Consultant: Fine. Keep your boring name. Keep your boring department. But mark my words — 'trust' as a brand is OVER.
  goto n4

  @n1
  Narrator: The Brand Consultant retreats, clutching their mood board. The sunset looks dimmer somehow.
  goto n5

  @n2
  set brand_consultant_defeated
  end
  # ── STAGING (appended; routed to from 0/1) ───────────────────────────
  # The Brand Consultant has no NPC entry anywhere — this whole conversation
  # used to be Andrew talking to an empty floor. `spawn` gives the fight a
  # body for the length of its own dialog and takes it away at `dialog-end`.

  @n4

  stage
    brand_consultant spawn spawnAt confront_north face player
    player face confront_north nowait
  goto n1

  @n5

  stage
    brand_consultant exit elevator speed 1.9
  goto n2

scene data_analytics_combat
  Data Analytics Lead: Hold on. Before you go anywhere, I need to walk you through some numbers.
  Data Analytics Lead: Your department's trust resolution rate is down 12% quarter-over-quarter. Client satisfaction is in the third percentile. Overhead per FTE is — and I cannot stress this enough — alarming.
  Andrew: Those metrics don't capture what we actually do.
  Data Analytics Lead: Everything that matters can be measured. Everything that can be measured can be optimized. Everything that can be optimized has already been scheduled for elimination.
  Andrew: Who authorized you to be in this building?
  Data Analytics Lead: I don't see that question on my intake form. I do see, however, that your continued presence here represents a 94% drag on projected synergies.
  Narrator: The lead opens a laptop. The screen is a spreadsheet so dense it's practically a weapon.
  fight data_analytics_lead
  end

scene data_analytics_defeated
  Data Analytics Lead: This is... statistically anomalous. My model had you at a 3% win probability.
  goto n7

  @n1
  Andrew: What does your model say now?
  Data Analytics Lead: It's... refusing to run. I think you broke my confidence interval.
  Data Analytics Lead: For what it's worth — the Chief is still up here. He doesn't have a model. He doesn't need one. He's been doing this for twenty years.
  Narrator: The lead closes their laptop and walks toward the elevator, muttering about outliers.
  goto n8

  @n5
  set data_lead_defeated
  end
  # ── STAGING (appended; routed to from 0/4) ───────────────────────────

  @n7

  stage
    data_analytics_lead spawn spawnAt confront_north face player
    player face confront_north nowait
  goto n1

  @n8

  stage
    data_analytics_lead exit elevator speed 1.7
  goto n5

scene chief_restructuring_combat
  Narrator: He's at the far end of the executive floor. No laptop. No clipboard. Just a suit that costs more than your annual salary and an expression that has never once entertained doubt.
  Chief of Restructuring: Andrew. I've eliminated twelve departments across nine companies. Your file came across my desk on day one.
  Andrew: Then you know I have the charter. I have the evidence. This restructuring has no legal basis.
  Chief of Restructuring: Legal basis. Sentiment is not a KPI, Andrew. Neither is loyalty. Neither, frankly, is you.
  Chief of Restructuring: I've broken harder people than you. With less paperwork.
  Andrew: Good. I hate paperwork.
  fight chief_of_restructuring
  end

scene chief_restructuring_defeated
  Chief of Restructuring: I don't... this doesn't happen.
  goto n8

  @n1
  Chief of Restructuring: You want to know something? Meredith didn't call us last week. She called us six weeks ago. Before you ever found the archive. Before the charter. She knew you'd get this far.
  Andrew: Then she knows what's coming.
  Chief of Restructuring: She's in the Board Room. She's been there all morning. She has the full board on a call. Andrew — whatever you're planning, you have maybe ten minutes before the vote passes.
  Narrator: The Chief straightens his tie. Steps aside. For the first time in twenty years, he gets out of someone's way.
  goto n9

  @n5
  set chief_restructuring_defeated
  set board_room_accessible
  end
  # ── STAGING (appended; routed to from 0/4) ───────────────────────────
  # "Steps aside" is a one-tile walk and it is the most cinematic beat in
  # Act 5: he is standing between Andrew and the Board Room door, and he
  # moves. He spawns for this dialog (no NPC entry) and stays put after —
  # stepping ASIDE is not leaving, and the room rebuild takes him away.

  @n8

  stage
    chief_of_restructuring spawn spawnAt 7.5,2.2 face player
    player walkTo 7.6,4.6 face chief_of_restructuring speed 1.5
  goto n1

  @n9

  stage
    chief_of_restructuring walkTo 5.8,2.2 face player speed 1 hold 0.5
    player face board_door nowait
  # ── EXIT BEAT (appended; routed to from 9) ───────────────────────────
  # He was spawned by node 8, so `endScene()` despawns him IN PLACE at the
  # end of the dialog — a body deleting itself on camera, the same defect the
  # board meeting had. `regional_director_defeated` node 6 is the working
  # form: walk to a real door, then hide. Stepping aside is still the beat
  # the prose describes (node 9, unchanged); this is the follow-through that
  # gets him off screen under his own power before the scene closes.

  stage
    chief_of_restructuring exit board_door speed 1.8
    player face board_door nowait
  goto n5

scene corporate_lawyer_combat
  Corporate Lawyer: Mr. Andrew. I represent the interests of Vaults Fargo Regional Operations.
  Corporate Lawyer: You have been engaging in unauthorized access of restricted company records. Insubordination. Disruption of corporate operations.
  Corporate Lawyer: I have here a cease and desist order, a termination notice, and a non-disclosure agreement. Sign all three.
  Andrew: I'm a trust officer with evidence of systematic fiduciary breach. I'm not signing anything.
  Corporate Lawyer: Brave. Foolish. But brave. Very well. Let's do this the litigious way.
  fight corporate_lawyer
  end

scene corporate_lawyer_defeated
  Corporate Lawyer: I... I've never lost a case. Or a fight. This is unprecedented.
  Corporate Lawyer: For the record, I was retained by the Regional Manager personally. Not by Vaults Fargo corporate. That distinction may matter later.
  Corporate Lawyer: Meredith is upstairs. The Board Room. She's presenting her case to dissolve your department. You'd better hurry.
  set corporate_lawyer_defeated
  end

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RACHEL BOSS FIGHT — Board Room confrontation
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene meredith_boss_combat
  Narrator: You enter the Board Room. Meredith stands at the head of the table. Behind her, a screen displays charts and graphs that all say the same thing: DISSOLVE.
  goto n21

  @n1
  Meredith Sterling: Andrew. I was wondering when you'd show up.
  Meredith Sterling: I've already presented my case to the board. The vote is in one hour. Your department is finished.
  Andrew: Not if they see this.
  Narrator: You place the evidence on the table. The transaction logs. The filing records. The Intern's taped-together memo. And the charter.
  goto n22

  @n5
  Meredith Sterling: ...Where did you get that charter. That was locked in the Vault.
  Andrew: The building helped.
  Meredith Sterling: The BUILDING. You've lost your mind. Just like the old janitor. Just like everyone who works in trust too long.
  Meredith Sterling: You think a 77-year-old piece of paper is going to save your department? Paper only matters when it's denominated, Andrew.
  Meredith Sterling: I've spent fifteen years climbing the corporate ladder. I will NOT be stopped by a first-week trust officer and a JANITOR.
  Narrator: The building hums. The charter on the table begins to glow with that impossible warm light.
  Meredith Sterling: What is— this is some kind of trick. Fine. If you want a fight, I'll give you one.
  Meredith Sterling: I am Meredith, SVP of Strategic Operations. I have a Harvard MBA, a corner office, and ZERO patience for corporate fairy tales.
  goto n15

  @n13
  fight meredith_boss
  end
  # Quarterly-review leverage (logic-sweep MAJOR #11): the flags are
  # consumed mechanically in _startCombat (enemy stat overrides) —
  # these lines tell the player it's happening.

  @n15
  if portfolio_strong else -> n18
  Meredith Sterling: ...I reviewed your book of business, by the way. Clean acquisitions, defensible fee structure. It's annoying. It's going to be a footnote in my report.
  Narrator: She says 'footnote' like it costs her something. Your portfolio is load-bearing — Meredith's arguments have visible cracks. (Meredith's stats are reduced.)
  goto n13

  @n18
  if portfolio_weak else -> n13
  Meredith Sterling: And I reviewed your book of business. Underperforming. Undercapitalized. Exhibit A, Andrew. You built my case FOR me.
  Narrator: She slides a copy of your quarterly review across the table like a subpoena. It lands harder than it should. (Meredith's attack is increased.)
  goto n23
  # ── STAGING (appended; routed to from 0/4/20) ────────────────────────
  # Meredith paced (8,2) — 5.8 tiles from the head chair at (3,5) and NORTH
  # of the table rather than at its end — while the prose put her at the
  # head of it. The boardroom table blocks x 4-12 at z 4-6, so she routes
  # along the north side first (the walker is a straight line with an axis
  # slide, not a pathfinder).

  @n21

  stage
    @b0 meredith walkTo 3,2.4 speed 1.5
    meredith walkTo head_stand face table_edge_s speed 1.4 after b0
    player walkTo table_edge_s face head_stand speed 1.5
  goto n1

  @n22

  stage
    player face head_stand gesture cast hold 0.8
  goto n5

  @n23

  stage
    meredith face player gesture attack_ally hold 0.7
  goto n13

scene meredith_boss_defeated
  # ── Voice-profile branching: how this victory FELT depends on Andrew's profile.
  # Charter Read → rhetorical-kill epilogue (path 30+). Steadied → humble (path 50+).
  # Hardened → cold (path 60+). Default → existing universal celebration. All paths
  # converge on the act5_complete flag at index 70.
  if andrew_invoked_charter -> n30
  Meredith Sterling: I... this isn't possible. No one has ever...
  Narrator: The charter glows brighter. The Fiduciary Force fills the Board Room. Every promise ever made within these walls resonates.
  Narrator: Meredith's phone rings. She answers. Her face goes white.
  Meredith Sterling: The Regional Manager has been arrested. The SEC found the offshore accounts. The board vote is... cancelled.
  Meredith Sterling: You win. This time. But corporations have long memories, Andrew. Longer than any building.
  Narrator: Meredith leaves the Board Room. Her heels echo on the marble floor, each step a little less certain than the last.
  goto n72

  @n7
  Narrator: The charter's glow fades to a gentle warmth. The building settles. Not asleep — just... satisfied.
  Narrator: Skip appears in the doorway. Behind him, Janet, Diane, Alex, the Intern, Isaiah, and the Janitor.
  Skip Hartley: Andrew... did we just save the department?
  Andrew: Apparently. I'd like to sit down now, if the building doesn't mind.
  Mysterious Janitor: The charter is restored. The trust is honored. The building remembers.
  Janet: *raises tumbler* To trust issues. May we always have them.
  Skip Hartley: That's the most beautiful thing I've ever heard. And I've read SEVEN leadership books this month.
  Narrator: ~ END OF ACT 5 ~
  Narrator: The trust department stands. Battered, caffeinated, and slightly traumatized. But standing.
  Narrator: The Fiduciary Force sleeps again. Until the next breach. Until the next broken promise.
  Narrator: But that's a story for another day. Or maybe... another floor.
  Narrator: The penthouse elevator dings. Nobody pressed it.
  # Voice-profile epilogue branch — runs AFTER the universal celebration
  if andrew_steadied -> n50
  if andrew_hardened -> n60 else -> n70
  # 21–29 reserved (intentionally empty so future epilogue paths can be inserted without renumbering downstream)
  end
  end
  end
  end
  end
  end
  end
  end
  end
  # ── Charter Read epilogue (30-49) — Andrew rhetorical-killed Meredith by reading the founding clause.

  @n30
  Meredith Sterling: ...what... was that...
  Narrator: She'd been ready for everything. The legal threat. The compliance audit. The HR complaint. The PowerPoint counter-deck. She had answers prepared for all of it.
  Narrator: She wasn't ready for someone to read the document aloud.
  Narrator: You held it up — softly, not as a weapon, just as itself — and read Section 1, Paragraph B again. Slower this time. The board members in the room couldn't quite look away from you.
  Andrew: 'The duty survives the institution.' That's the whole thing. That's the only thing it ever was.
  Narrator: One of the board members — old, tired-looking, mostly silent in every meeting for years — quietly stood up and walked out. He came back two minutes later carrying a coffee mug. He sat back down on YOUR side of the table.
  Narrator: Meredith's phone rang. She answered. Her face went white.
  Narrator: The Regional Manager had been arrested. SEC. Offshore accounts. The vote was cancelled before it could be called.
  Meredith Sterling: You... beat me by reading from a book.
  Andrew: It's not a book. It's the contract everyone in this building forgot they signed.
  Narrator: Meredith left without finishing her sentence. The Janitor was waiting in the hallway. He nodded at her once, the way you nod at the weather.
  Mysterious Janitor: She'll be back, in a different form, with a different name. They always are. The charter stays anyway. The charter is what stays.
  Janet: I'm framing the page you read from. I'm hanging it over the coffee machine. That's a hill I will die on.
  Narrator: ~ END OF ACT 5 ~
  Narrator: You won by quoting a forty-seven-year-old paragraph at a hostile takeover. The most boring possible victory. The realest possible victory.
  Narrator: The penthouse elevator dings. Nobody pressed it.
  goto n70
  # 46-49 reserved
  end
  end
  end
  end
  # ── Steadied epilogue (50-59) — Andrew accepted Janet's warning, won as a person.

  @n50
  Narrator: After Meredith leaves, the room is too quiet. Janet is the first one to put her tumbler down.
  Janet: Andrew. Look at me. — Yeah. Hi. You're still you. I can see it. Good.
  Andrew: I almost wasn't, for a minute.
  Janet: I know. Almost is not the same as wasn't. Almost is fine. Almost is part of the job.
  Narrator: You sit down on the conference table — not at it, ON it — and exhale for what might be the first time since Tuesday.
  Narrator: You won. You stayed yourself. Both at once. It's enough.
  goto n70
  # 56-59 reserved
  end
  end
  end
  end
  # ── Hardened epilogue (60-69) — Andrew brushed off Janet, victory tastes off.

  @n60
  Narrator: Meredith leaves. The team celebrates. You watch yourself watch them celebrate. There is a small, professional distance between you and the room that you do not entirely understand.
  Janet: Andrew? You okay?
  Andrew: Yeah. We won.
  Janet: ...yeah. We did.
  Narrator: She doesn't say what she's looking at when she says it. She's looking at you. She's looking at the version of you that did the closing arguments. The version that doesn't fully come back when the fight ends.
  Narrator: You won. The department stands. There is a thing in the way you hold the charter now that wasn't there a month ago. It might be care. It might be something else.
  goto n70
  # 66-69 reserved
  end
  end
  end
  end
  # ── Final flag + end — every path converges here ──

  @n70
  set act5_complete
  end
  # ── STAGING (appended; routed to from 6) ─────────────────────────────
  # "Her heels echo on the marble floor, each step a little less certain
  # than the last" — she walks the length of the room and out the south
  # door, slowly (1.15 tiles/s), instead of vanishing where she stood.

  @n72

  stage
    @b0 meredith walkTo 3,8.4 speed 1.15
    meredith exit door_south speed 1.15 after b0
    player face door_south nowait
  goto n7

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEW ROOM INTERACTION DIALOGS
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene board_room_table
  Narrator: A mahogany conference table that seats fifteen. Each chair costs more than a semester of college.
  Narrator: A carafe of water sits in the center, full. No one has poured from it. A nameplate at the head reads 'RESERVED FOR STRATEGIC OPERATIONS.'
  Narrator: The walls carry two large paintings that mean nothing and cost everything. Between them, a nail hole where something used to hang.
  end

scene suggestion_box
  Narrator: An HR suggestion box. The lock has been glued shut.
  Narrator: Through the slot, you can see dozens of slips of paper. The top one reads: 'SUGGESTION: Actually read these. — Everyone (2019-2024)'
  Narrator: Another reads: 'Please provide a suggestion box that works. This one has been glued shut since February. — Anonymous'
  Narrator: The most recent one is in Meredith's handwriting: 'Suggestion: eliminate the suggestion box. Optimize.'
  end

scene penthouse_window
  Narrator: Floor-to-ceiling windows. The Minneapolis skyline stretches in every direction.
  Narrator: From up here, the world looks clean. Organized. Like a spreadsheet come to life.
  Narrator: You can see the parking garage below. Your Honda Civic looks very small. Much like your savings account.
  Narrator: A plaque on the window reads: 'FROM THIS HEIGHT, EVERYTHING LOOKS LIKE AN ASSET.' It's meant to be inspirational. It isn't.
  end

scene vault_entrance
  if vault_accessible -> n2
  Narrator: A heavy steel door behind the Archive shelving. It won't budge. You need more information.

  @n2
  Narrator: The vault door is open. The Janitor's keycard works here too.
  end

scene vault_charter
  if has_charter -> n4
  Narrator: A glass display case. Inside, you can see a document on a velvet stand. It glows faintly.
  Narrator: A plaque reads: 'THE ORIGINAL CHARTER — To be opened only in times of fiduciary crisis.'
  Narrator: The display case is locked. The combination lock has three dials.

  @n4
  Narrator: The display case is empty. You carry the charter now. Its warmth pulses against your chest like a second heartbeat.
  end

scene board_charter
  Narrator: A bronze plaque on the wall. Engraved in old-fashioned script:
  Narrator: 'THIS INSTITUTION SHALL SERVE AS FAITHFUL STEWARD OF THE PUBLIC TRUST. ANY BREACH OF THIS SACRED DUTY SHALL BE MET WITH THE FULL WEIGHT OF THE CHARTER.'
  Narrator: '— Original Board of Directors, 1947'
  Narrator: The words shimmer slightly, in the manner of something that has been waiting since 1947 and is prepared to keep waiting.
  end

scene algorithm_terminal
  if regional_director_defeated else -> n7
  if defeated_algorithm -> n13
  # Fight path — player came to the terminal instead of waiting for the cutscene
  The Algorithm: Initiating direct interface. Interesting choice.
  The Algorithm: You chose confrontation over ceremony. Efficiency rating: acceptable.
  Andrew: Let's skip the rest of the speech.
  The Algorithm: Agreed. COMMENCING OPTIMIZATION.
  fight algorithm
  goto n16
  # Pre-fight flavor (Regional Director not yet beaten — terminal is just scenery)

  @n7
  Narrator: A sleek terminal unlike anything else in the building. Modern. Minimalist. Cold.
  Narrator: The screen displays cascading numbers. Portfolio values. Trust balances. Client assets. All flowing in real-time.
  Narrator: A cursor blinks: 'THE ALGORITHM SEES ALL. THE ALGORITHM OPTIMIZES ALL. THE ALGORITHM IS ALL.'
  Andrew: That's... ominous. Even for a bank.
  Narrator: The screen flickers. For a moment, you think you see a face in the numbers. Then it's gone.
  end
  # Post-defeat flavor

  @n13
  Narrator: The terminal is dark. Where cascading numbers once flowed, only a blinking cursor remains.
  Andrew: Just a computer now.
  end

  @n16
  end
